Programming in JavaScript<P>
<HR>
<H1>About this book</H1>
<HR>
This book teaches you to program in JavaScript by writing programs for RISC OS: programs that print and ask
questions, open windows, draw pictures, respond to the mouse, animate, have menus and icons on the icon bar,
load and save files, and finally a complete game.<P>

<A NAME=S1></A>
<H2>Who this book is for</H2>
You don't need to be an expert. The book assumes you have written a few lines of code before, in any
language - perhaps some BBC BASIC at school, or a little Python - and so you know roughly what a
<I>variable</I>, a <I>loop</I> and an <I>if</I> are. It doesn't assume you have written much more than that.
Every new idea is explained with a small example before it is used.<P>
You should know your way around the RISC OS desktop: how to open directories, start applications and use
menus. If you don't, chapter 1 of the <A HREF="file://ADFS::HardDisc4/$/Manuals/Manual/BOOKB/BOOK_3.HTM">RISC OS
3.7 User Guide</A> explains it.<P>

<A NAME=S2></A>
<H2>What JavaScript is</H2>
JavaScript is one of the most widely used programming languages in the world. It was invented in 1995 to make
web pages do things, and today it runs almost everywhere: in web browsers, on servers, in phones and in
desktop applications. What you learn here is ordinary, modern JavaScript and is useful anywhere.<P>
It is also the language this RISC OS desktop itself is written in. Edit, Draw, Paint, the games in
Diversions and the rest were all written in JavaScript, using the same programming interface this book
teaches. When you have finished the book you will be writing programs the same way they were.<P>
<B>Note</B>: on a real Acorn computer, RISC OS programs were written in BBC BASIC, C or ARM assembly
language. JavaScript programs run here because this desktop has a JavaScript engine to run them with.<P>

<A NAME=S3></A>
<H2>What you need</H2>
Nothing more than this desktop. You type your programs in Edit, save them as <I>JSScript</I> files and
double-click them to run them - just as you would run a BASIC program. Chapter 1 shows you how.<P>
Your programs are kept on the hard disc, which remembers them between visits (it is stored in your web
browser). If you would rather keep them on your own computer, and perhaps edit them with another editor,
appendix D explains how to use HostFS to do that.<P>

<A NAME=S4></A>
<H2>How the book is arranged</H2>
<UL>
<LI>Chapters 1 to 5 teach the JavaScript language itself: values, variables, decisions, loops, functions,
arrays and objects. The programs print text and ask questions in a simple window.<P>
<LI>Chapters 6 to 10 teach desktop programming: windows, drawing, the mouse and keyboard, animation, menus
and the icon bar.<P>
<LI>Chapters 11 and 12 show how to organise larger programs into modules, and how to load and save files.<P>
<LI>Chapter 13 puts everything together in a complete game, Snake, packaged as a proper RISC OS
application.<P>
<LI>The appendices are quick references: the JavaScript language, the RISC OS programming interface, error
messages, and tips on using Edit.<P>
</UL>
Each chapter ends with a summary and some exercises, with answers. The exercises are the best way to find out
whether an idea has sunk in: try them before looking at the answers.<P>

<A NAME=S5></A>
<H2>The example programs</H2>
Every program in the book is in the directory <CODE>HardDisc4.$.Examples.JS</CODE>, under the name the book
gives it. Double-click one to run it; Shift-double-click it to open it in Edit and read it. If you want to
change an example, save your copy somewhere else first, so the original is always there to go back to.<P>

<A NAME=S6></A>
<H2>Conventions used in this book</H2>
<DL>
<DT>Program text
<DD>JavaScript, and anything you type, is shown like this: <CODE>print('Hello');</CODE><P>
<DT>Mouse buttons
<DD>As in the User Guide, the three mouse buttons are called Select, Menu and Adjust.<P>
<DT>Menus
<DD>A route through menus is written like this: <I>Menu &gt; Misc &gt; Set type</I>, meaning click Menu, move
to Misc, then to Set type.<P>
</DL>

